yield: for a 2kg boneless shoulder — serves 6-8 sandwiches
uses:
  - sourdough
  - slaw
  - quick-pickles
ingredients:
  - item: pork shoulder
    mass: 2000
    basis: true
    note: boneless, skin off, fat cap trimmed to ~5mm; bone-in works, add ~1hr
components:
  - name: rub
    ingredients:
      - item: cooking salt
        mass: 20
        note: 1% of meat weight; kosher salt — reduce 25% for table salt, increase 50% for flakes
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 40
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub white
      - item: smoked paprika
        mass: 15
        qty: 2
        unit: tbsp
        note: sub sweet paprika
      - item: garlic powder
        mass: 9
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: onion powder
        mass: 6
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: mustard powder
        mass: 5.5
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: cumin powder
        mass: 4
        qty: 2
        unit: tsp
      - item: coarse black pepper
        mass: 4
        qty: 1
        unit: tbsp
      - item: cayenne
        mass: 0.4
        qty: 0.5
        unit: tsp
        optional: true
        note: for spice
    steps:
      - salt first, then the rest of the mix — all faces, into the crevices
  - name: mop
    ingredients:
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 120
      - item: apple cider
        mass: 120
        note: or apple juice
    steps:
      - combine in a sprayer
  - name: finishing sauce (carolina vinegar)
    ingredients:
      - item: apple cider vinegar
        mass: 240
      - item: water
        mass: 60
      - item: tomato sauce
        mass: 60
      - item: brown sugar
        mass: 30
      - item: salt
        mass: 5
      - item: chili flakes
        mass: 2
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
      - item: black pepper
        mass: 2
        qty: 1
        unit: tsp
      - item: hot sauce
        optional: true
        note: few dashes
    steps:
      - whisk everything cold until sugar and salt dissolve — no cooking needed
      - rest at least 30 mins for the chili to bloom
  - name: procedure (oven)
    steps:
      - pat dry, apply rub, refrigerate uncovered overnight (4 hrs minimum)
      - out of fridge 1 hr before cooking
      - oven to 135°C, wire rack over a water-filled tray to catch drippings
      - fat cap up, uncovered, until core hits 70-75°C and the bark is set and dark (3-4 hrs)
      - spray with mop every hour once the surface looks dry
      - wrap tight in foil (or butcher paper) with a splash of mop inside
      - back in until core reaches 96°C and a skewer slides in with no resistance (another 3-4 hrs)
      - rest wrapped 1 hr — this is not optional, it reabsorbs the juices
      - unwrap over a bowl, keep the rendered juices
      - pull with two forks or claws, discarding hard fat and gristle
      - toss the pulled meat with the reserved juices first, then finishing sauce to taste
  - name: procedure (slow-cooker)
    steps:
      - rub as above, sear all faces hard in a pan first (the slow cooker won't build bark)
      - 8 hrs on low with 100g of the mop in the bottom, no other liquid
      - lift out, pull, then reduce the cooking liquid in a pan before tossing it through
    notes: |
      Easier, but you trade the bark for it. Worth spreading the pulled meat on a tray and
      blasting under a hot grill for 5-10 mins to get some crisp edges back.
  - name: assembly
    steps:
      - thick slices of sourdough, toasted hard — it has to hold up to wet pork
      - butter the toast while hot
      - pile on pork, pickles over the pork, slaw over the top so it doesn't steam
      - open-faced, or top slice on for a proper sandwich
notes: |
  Shoulder (aka pork butt / boston butt) only. Leg or loin will not pull — not enough
  collagen or intramuscular fat, they go stringy and dry at the temps this needs.

  The target isn't doneness, it's collagen breakdown. 96°C core held there long enough is
  what turns connective tissue to gelatin; a shoulder pulled at 85°C is cooked but won't
  shred. Go by probe feel over the thermometer at the end — no resistance anywhere.

  The stall around 70-75°C is evaporative cooling, not a problem. Wrapping pushes through
  it and stops the bark going leathery. Foil is faster and softens the bark; butcher paper
  is slower and keeps it crisper.

  Carolina vinegar sauce over a thick sweet bbq sauce, deliberately — the pork is already
  rich and the toast is already carby. Acid is what makes the sandwich eat well instead of
  sitting on you. Same reason the slaw is sharp.

  Faster oven route: 160°C covered for ~5 hrs. Fine, but you lose most of the bark.
